  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 259 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nProbabilities for showers and thunderstorms remain low across much \nof the area Friday with limited chances (15-20%) along the northern \nfringes of the CWA. Heat rebounds over the weekend with chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms returning next week.\n\nThe active convective pattern that has persisted across parts of the \nregion will persist through the next several days. The key will be \nthe anomalously strong ridge that is centered near the Gulf Coast, \nalong with surface features (e.g. outflow) induced by convective \nbehavior rounding the northern periphery of the ridge. NBM blends \nhang onto slight chance PoPs over the far northern sections of the \nCWA, which is reasonable considering the uncertainty involved with \nthese small features. LREF ensembles show 500mb mean heights \nreaching near the 100th percentile, which slightly flattens as it \nexpands westward this weekend into next week. The initial impact is \nthe return of excessive heat with high well into the 90s and heat \nindex values approach 110F in some locations Friday through Sunday. \nLREFs exceedance probabilities indicate a 60-70% chance for apparent \ntemperatures at or above 100 along and south of I-70. Even KUIN \nexceeds 50%, though this region of the CWA stands the best chance \nfor adjustment given the active pattern to the north. If a shower or \nthunderstorm makes it far enough south, it could at least briefly \nalleviate some of the heat. By early next week, the center of the \nridge shift westward into the southern Plains with a NW/SE \norientation to the northern side of the ridge. Global guidance \nshows multiple shortwaves rounding the ridge and could be an \nearly sign of shifting the MCS track southward, though the exact\ntrack of these features is rarely certain this far out. The \ngreatest sensible impact, aside from thunderstorm chances, will \nbe cooler daytime temperatures. \n\nHigh temperatures remain in the 90s through much of the period with \nurban areas inching close to 100 degrees Saturday and Sunday. Low \ntemperatures will largely be in the 70s.",
